Calculate net receipts from gross receipts minus returns and allowances. Form 1120 line 1c = 1a - 1b
revenue.gross_receipts > 0 Has gross receipts from sales
Y
revenue.net_receipts = revenue.gross_receipts - revenue.returns_and_allowances;
Net receipts = Gross receipts - Returns and allowances. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 1c
N
revenue.net_receipts = 0.0;
No gross receipts = zero net receipts.
Calculate gross profit from net receipts minus cost of goods sold. Form 1120 line 3 = 1c - 2
revenue.net_receipts > 0 Has net receipts from sales
Y
revenue.gross_profit = revenue.net_receipts - revenue.cost_of_goods_sold;
Gross profit = Net receipts - Cost of goods sold. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 3 Reference: Schedule A - Cost of Goods Sold
N
revenue.gross_profit = 0.0 - revenue.cost_of_goods_sold;
No receipts: gross profit = negative COGS (loss from inventory).
Calculate total income (sum of all income sources). Form 1120 line 11
true Always calculate total income
Y
revenue.total_income = revenue.gross_profit + revenue.dividends_received + revenue.interest_income + revenue.gross_rents + revenue.gross_royalties + revenue.capital_gain_net_income + revenue.net_gain_loss_4797 + revenue.other_income;
// Copy to result entity for tax calculation result.total_income = revenue.total_income;
Total income = Gross profit (line 3) + Dividends (line 4) + Interest (line 5) + Rents (line 6) + Royalties (line 7) + Capital gain (line 8) + Net gain/loss Form 4797 (line 9) + Other income (line 10). Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 11
Calculate deduction for officer compensation. Form 1120 line 12
result.compensation > 0 Has officer compensation expense
Y
// Officer compensation is fully deductible (subject to reasonableness test) // Reasonableness test is typically applied during audit, not in calculation
Compensation paid to officers is deductible under IRC Section 162(a)(1). Must be reasonable in amount and for services actually performed. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 12 Reference: IRC Section 162(a)(1) Reference: Reg. Section 1.162-7 (Compensation for personal services)
N
result.compensation = 0.0;
No officer compensation to deduct.
Calculate interest expense deduction with IRC 163(j) limitation. Form 1120 line 18
result.interest_expense > 0 Has interest expense
result.interest_expense > (0.30 * result.taxable_income) Interest expense exceeds 30% of adjusted taxable income
Y N
// Interest expense is fully deductible (under 163(j) limit)
Business interest expense is fully deductible when it does not exceed business interest income plus 30% of adjusted taxable income. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 18 Reference: IRC Section 163(j)
Y Y
// Limit interest deduction to 30% of ATI // Excess carried forward (not implemented in Phase 1) result.interest_expense = 0.30 * result.taxable_income;
Business interest expense limited to 30% of adjusted taxable income per IRC 163(j). Excess interest carried forward indefinitely. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 18 Reference: IRC Section 163(j) Reference: Form 8990 - Limitation on Business Interest Expense
N
result.interest_expense = 0.0;
No interest expense to deduct.
Calculate charitable contributions deduction with 10% limitation. Form 1120 line 19
result.charitable_contributions > 0 Has charitable contributions
result.charitable_contributions > (0.10 * result.taxable_income) Contributions exceed 10% of taxable income
Y N
// Charitable contributions fully deductible (under 10% limit)
Charitable contributions are fully deductible when they do not exceed 10% of taxable income (computed without the charitable deduction). Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 19 Reference: IRC Section 170(b)(2)
Y Y
// Limit charitable contributions to 10% of taxable income // Excess carried forward 5 years (not implemented in Phase 1) result.charitable_contributions = 0.10 * result.taxable_income;
Charitable contributions limited to 10% of taxable income per IRC 170(b)(2). Excess contributions carried forward 5 years. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 19 Reference: IRC Section 170(b)(2)
N
result.charitable_contributions = 0.0;
No charitable contributions to deduct.
Calculate total deductions (sum of all deduction line items). Form 1120 line 27
true Always calculate total deductions
Y
result.total_deductions = result.compensation + result.salaries_and_wages + result.repairs_and_maintenance + result.bad_debts + result.rents + result.taxes_and_licenses + result.interest_expense + result.charitable_contributions + result.depreciation + result.depletion + result.advertising + result.pension_profit_sharing + result.employee_benefit_programs + result.other_deductions;
Total deductions = Sum of lines 12-26 (all deduction categories). Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 27
Calculate taxable income (total income minus total deductions). Form 1120 line 28 = 11 - 27
result.total_income > result.total_deductions Income exceeds deductions
Y
result.taxable_income = result.total_income - result.total_deductions;
Taxable income = Total income - Total deductions. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 28
N
result.taxable_income = 0.0; // Net operating loss (NOL) - Phase 4
Deductions exceed income: taxable income is zero. Net operating loss (NOL) may be available for carryforward/carryback. Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Line 28 Reference: IRC Section 172 (NOL deduction)
Calculate federal corporate income tax at 21% flat rate. Form 1120 line 31 = line 28 × 21%
result.taxable_income > 0 Has positive taxable income
Y
result.federal_tax = result.taxable_income * result.federal_tax_rate;
Federal corporate income tax = Taxable income × 21% (flat rate). Rate established by Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (TCJA) of 2017, effective 2018+. No progressive brackets. No Alternative Minimum Tax (AMT repealed for corporations). Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Schedule J Line 2 Reference: IRC Section 11(b) - Tax imposed on corporations
N
result.federal_tax = 0.0;
No taxable income = no tax liability.
Calculate final refund or amount owed. Payments + Credits - Tax
true Always calculate final amount
Y
// Total payments includes estimated payments + prior year overpayment credits result.total_payments = result.estimated_tax_payments + result.total_credits;
// Refund (positive) or owed (negative) result.refund_or_owed = result.total_payments - result.federal_tax;
If (Payments + Credits) > Tax: Refund If (Payments + Credits) < Tax: Amount owed Reference: Form 1120 Instructions, Lines 32-35 Line 32: Total payments and refundable credits Line 33: Estimated tax penalty (not implemented Phase 1) Line 34: Amount owed = Tax - Payments (if positive) Line 35: Overpayment = Payments - Tax (if positive)
Calculate research and development tax credit. Form 6765 - Credit for Increasing Research Activities
result.research_development_credit > 0 Has qualified research expenses (QREs)
Y
// R&D credit calculation (simplified for Phase 2) // Full implementation requires base period calculations // Phase 2: Accept pre-calculated credit amount
Research & Development credit = 20% of qualified research expenses (QREs) exceeding base amount.
QREs include: - Wages for qualified research activities - Supplies used in research - Contract research expenses (65% of amount paid)
Base amount calculation requires 4-year average of prior QREs.
Reference: Form 6765 - Credit for Increasing Research Activities Reference: IRC Section 41 - Credit for increasing research activities Reference: Publication 561 - Determining Value of Donated Property
N
result.research_development_credit = 0.0;
No qualified research expenses = no R&D credit.
Calculate general business credit (aggregate of all credits). Form 3800 - General Business Credit
result.research_development_credit > 0 || result.work_opportunity_credit > 0 Has at least one business credit
Y
// Sum all business credits result.general_business_credit = result.research_development_credit + result.work_opportunity_credit;
// Apply to total credits result.total_credits = result.general_business_credit;
General business credit = Sum of all component credits - Research & development credit (Form 6765) - Work opportunity tax credit (Form 5884) - Energy credits (not implemented) - Low-income housing credit (not implemented) - Other credits (not implemented)
Credit is limited to net income tax minus greater of: (1) Tentative minimum tax, or (2) 25% of net regular tax liability above $25,000
Unused credits carry back 1 year, forward 20 years.
Reference: Form 3800 Instructions Reference: IRC Section 38 - General business credit
N
result.general_business_credit = 0.0; result.total_credits = 0.0;
No business credits available.
Determine MACRS asset class and recovery period for depreciable property. Publication 946 - How to Depreciate Property
asset.asset_class != null && asset.asset_class != '' Asset class is already specified
Y
// Asset class already specified, set recovery period if (asset.asset_class == '3-year') { asset.recovery_period = 3; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-200DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '5-year') { asset.recovery_period = 5; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-200DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '7-year') { asset.recovery_period = 7; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-200DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '10-year') { asset.recovery_period = 10; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-200DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '15-year') { asset.recovery_period = 15; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-150DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '20-year') { asset.recovery_period = 20; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-150DB'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '27.5-year') { asset.recovery_period = 27.5; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-SL'; } else if (asset.asset_class == '39-year') { asset.recovery_period = 39; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-SL'; }
MACRS asset classification determines recovery period and depreciation method.
Property Classes (Publication 946 Table B-1): - 3-year: Tractor units, racehorses 2+ years, breeding hogs - 5-year: Automobiles, computers, office equipment, breeding/dairy cattle - 7-year: Office furniture, fixtures, most machinery and equipment - 10-year: Vessels, barges, tugs, single-purpose agricultural structures - 15-year: Land improvements, gas stations, depreciable improvements - 20-year: Farm buildings, municipal sewers - 27.5-year: Residential rental property - 39-year: Nonresidential real property (commercial buildings)
Depreciation methods: - 200% declining balance (200DB) for 3, 5, 7, 10-year property - 150% declining balance (150DB) for 15, 20-year property - Straight-line (SL) for 27.5, 39-year property
Reference: Publication 946 - How to Depreciate Property Reference: IRC Section 168 - MACRS
N
// Default to 7-year property if not specified asset.asset_class = '7-year'; asset.recovery_period = 7; asset.depreciation_method = 'MACRS-200DB';
If asset class not specified, default to 7-year property (most common).
Calculate Section 179 expense deduction for qualifying property. Form 4562 Part I - Election To Expense Certain Property
asset.eligible_for_section_179 == true Property qualifies for Section 179
result.section_179_total < result.section_179_limit Haven't exceeded annual Section 179 limit
asset.cost_basis > 0 Has remaining basis to expense
Y Y Y
// Calculate available Section 179 deduction double remaining_limit = result.section_179_limit - result.section_179_total; double section_179_amount = Math.min(asset.cost_basis, remaining_limit);
// Apply Section 179 deduction asset.section_179_deduction = section_179_amount; result.section_179_total += section_179_amount;
// Reduce basis for remaining depreciation asset.cost_basis -= section_179_amount;
Section 179 expense deduction allows immediate expensing of qualifying property.
2025 Limits: - Maximum deduction: $1,220,000 - Phaseout threshold: $3,050,000 (total property placed in service) - Phaseout: Dollar-for-dollar reduction above threshold
Qualifying property: - Tangible personal property (machinery, equipment, furniture, computers) - Off-the-shelf software - Qualified improvement property (QIP) - Must be used > 50% for business
Nonqualifying property: - Buildings and structural components - Land and land improvements - Property used outside the U.S. - Property used for lodging - Listed property used 50% or less for business
Limitation: Cannot exceed taxable income from business.
Reference: Form 4562 Instructions, Part I Reference: IRC Section 179 - Election to expense certain depreciable business assets Reference: Publication 946 Chapter 2
N
asset.section_179_deduction = 0.0;
Property does not qualify for Section 179 deduction.
Y N
asset.section_179_deduction = 0.0;
Section 179 annual limit exceeded. No additional deduction available.
Calculate bonus depreciation for qualifying property. Form 4562 Part II - Special Depreciation Allowance
asset.eligible_for_bonus == true Property qualifies for bonus depreciation
result.bonus_depreciation_rate > 0 Bonus depreciation rate is greater than zero
asset.cost_basis > 0 Has remaining basis after Section 179
Y Y Y
// Calculate bonus depreciation double bonus_amount = asset.cost_basis * result.bonus_depreciation_rate;
// Apply bonus depreciation asset.bonus_depreciation = bonus_amount; result.bonus_depreciation_total += bonus_amount;
// Reduce basis for MACRS depreciation asset.cost_basis -= bonus_amount;
Bonus depreciation allows additional first-year depreciation for qualifying property.
Phaseout schedule (TCJA): - 2022: 100% - 2023: 80% - 2024: 60% - 2025: 40% - 2026: 20% - 2027: 0% (expires)
Qualifying property: - New or used property (expanded by TCJA) - Original use begins with taxpayer OR acquired used property - MACRS property with recovery period of 20 years or less - Computer software - Qualified improvement property (QIP) - Placed in service during tax year
Nonqualifying property: - Property required to use Alternative Depreciation System (ADS) - Listed property used 50% or less for business - Property used for lodging
Election to opt out: Taxpayer can elect out of bonus depreciation.
Reference: Form 4562 Instructions, Part II Reference: IRC Section 168(k) - Special allowance for certain property Reference: Publication 946 Chapter 3
N
asset.bonus_depreciation = 0.0;
Property does not qualify for bonus depreciation.
Y N
asset.bonus_depreciation = 0.0;
Bonus depreciation rate is 0% for 2025 (phased out).
Calculate MACRS depreciation for property. Form 4562 Part III - MACRS Depreciation
asset.cost_basis > 0 Has remaining basis after Section 179 and bonus
asset.accumulated_depreciation == 0 First year of depreciation
Y Y
// Calculate first-year MACRS depreciation using half-year convention double macrs_rate = 0.0;
// Lookup MACRS percentage (simplified - using approximate rates) if (asset.recovery_period == 3) { macrs_rate = 0.3333; // 33.33% first year (200DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 5) { macrs_rate = 0.2000; // 20.00% first year (200DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 7) { macrs_rate = 0.1429; // 14.29% first year (200DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 10) { macrs_rate = 0.1000; // 10.00% first year (200DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 15) { macrs_rate = 0.0500; // 5.00% first year (150DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 20) { macrs_rate = 0.0375; // 3.75% first year (150DB, half-year) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 27.5) { macrs_rate = 0.0182; // 1.82% first year (SL, mid-month) } else if (asset.recovery_period == 39) { macrs_rate = 0.0128; // 1.28% first year (SL, mid-month) }
// Calculate depreciation double macrs_amount = asset.cost_basis * macrs_rate; asset.current_year_depreciation = macrs_amount; asset.accumulated_depreciation += macrs_amount; result.macrs_depreciation_total += macrs_amount;
MACRS (Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System) depreciation using IRS tables.
Depreciation methods by property class: - 3, 5, 7, 10-year: 200% declining balance (200DB) - 15, 20-year: 150% declining balance (150DB) - 27.5, 39-year: Straight-line (SL)
Conventions: - Half-year: Property placed in service mid-year (most property) - Mid-quarter: If > 40% of property placed in service in Q4 - Mid-month: Residential/nonresidential real property
First-year rates (half-year convention): - 3-year: 33.33% - 5-year: 20.00% - 7-year: 14.29% - 10-year: 10.00% - 15-year: 5.00% - 20-year: 3.75%
Full MACRS tables in Publication 946 Appendix A.
Reference: Form 4562 Instructions, Part III Reference: IRC Section 168 - MACRS Reference: Publication 946 Appendix A (MACRS Percentage Tables)
N
asset.current_year_depreciation = 0.0;
No remaining basis to depreciate (fully expensed via Section 179/bonus).
Aggregate all depreciation deductions for Form 1120. Form 4562 - Depreciation and Amortization
true Always aggregate depreciation
Y
// Total depreciation = Section 179 + Bonus + MACRS result.depreciation = result.section_179_total + result.bonus_depreciation_total + result.macrs_depreciation_total;
Total depreciation deduction (Form 1120 line 20) equals: - Section 179 expense deduction (Form 4562 Part I) - Bonus depreciation (Form 4562 Part II) - MACRS depreciation (Form 4562 Part III) - Amortization (not implemented)
Reference: Form 4562 Line 22 Reference: Form 1120 Line 20
