Corporate tax calculation flow (Phase 4 - with schedules):
1. Calculate income (Form 1120 lines 1-11) 2. Calculate depreciation (Form 4562): a. Section 179 expense deduction b. Bonus depreciation (phased out in 2025) c. MACRS depreciation 3. Calculate deductions (Form 1120 lines 12-27) 4. Calculate taxable income (Form 1120 line 28) 5. Apply NOL deduction (IRC 172): a. NOL carryforward from prior years b. 80% limitation c. Current year NOL generation 6. Apply 21% tax rate (Schedule J) 7. Calculate credits (Form 3800): a. R&D credit (Form 6765) b. General business credit 8. Apply foreign tax credit (Form 1118): a. FTC limitation calculation b. Carryback/carryforward tracking 9. Determine refund or amount owed 10. Complete Schedule M-1 (Book-tax reconciliation) 11. Complete Schedule M-2 (Retained earnings analysis) 12. Complete Schedule L (Balance sheet per books)
Reference: Form 1120 - U.S. Corporation Income Tax Return Reference: Form 4562 - Depreciation and Amortization Reference: Form 3800 - General Business Credit Reference: Form 6765 - Research & Development Credit Reference: Form 1118 - Foreign Tax Credit Reference: Form 1120 Schedule M-1 - Reconciliation of Income Reference: Form 1120 Schedule M-2 - Analysis of Retained Earnings Reference: Form 1120 Schedule L - Balance Sheet per Books Reference: IRC Section 172 - Net Operating Loss Deduction Reference: IRC Section 901 - Foreign Tax Credit
Determine if a foreign corporation qualifies as a Passive Foreign Investment Company (PFIC) under IRC § 1297.
A corporation is a PFIC if it meets either: 1. Income Test: 75% or more of gross income is passive income (IRC § 1297(a)(1)) 2. Asset Test: 50% or more of assets produce or are held to produce passive income (IRC § 1297(a)(2))
Passive income includes: dividends, interest, rents, royalties, annuities, gains from commodities/securities, foreign currency gains.
Exception: CFC stock held by U.S. shareholders may be exempt under IRC § 1297(d).
Reference: IRC § 1297, Form 8621 Instructions
// Calculate passive income components pfic.passive_income = pfic.dividends_received + pfic.interest_income + pfic.rents_received + pfic.royalties_received + pfic.annuities_received + pfic.commodities_gains + pfic.securities_gains + pfic.foreign_currency_gains + pfic.other_passive_income;
// Calculate income test percentage if (pfic.gross_income_total > 0) { pfic.passive_income_percentage = pfic.passive_income / pfic.gross_income_total; pfic.income_test_met = (pfic.passive_income_percentage >= 0.75); } else { pfic.passive_income_percentage = 0.0; pfic.income_test_met = false; }
// Calculate asset test percentage if (pfic.total_assets_value > 0) { pfic.passive_assets_percentage = pfic.passive_assets_value / pfic.total_assets_value; pfic.asset_test_met = (pfic.passive_assets_percentage >= 0.50); } else { pfic.passive_assets_percentage = 0.0; pfic.asset_test_met = false; }
// Calculate ownership percentage if (pfic.shares_outstanding > 0) { pfic.ownership_percentage = pfic.shares_owned / pfic.shares_outstanding; } else { pfic.ownership_percentage = 0.0; }
Y
// CFC exception: U.S. shareholders not subject to PFIC rules for CFC stock pfic.is_cfc = true; pfic.cfc_exception_applies = true; pfic.is_pfic = false; pfic.form_8621_required = false;
CFC Exception to PFIC Rules (IRC § 1297(d)).
If a foreign corporation qualifies as a Controlled Foreign Corporation (CFC), U.S. shareholders are generally not subject to PFIC taxation rules for their CFC stock. Subpart F and GILTI regimes apply instead.
This prevents double taxation under both PFIC and CFC rules.
Reference: IRC § 1297(d), IRC § 951-965 (Subpart F)
N Y
// Income test met: 75%+ of gross income is passive pfic.is_pfic = true; pfic.form_8621_required = true;
PFIC Income Test Met (IRC § 1297(a)(1)).
Corporation qualifies as PFIC because 75% or more of its gross income is passive income (dividends, interest, rents, royalties, etc.).
U.S. shareholders must file Form 8621 and choose taxation method: - Default excess distribution regime (harsh treatment with interest charge) - QEF election (current inclusion of earnings) - Mark-to-market election (if stock is marketable)
Reference: IRC § 1297(a)(1), Form 8621
N N Y
// Asset test met: 50%+ of assets produce passive income pfic.is_pfic = true; pfic.form_8621_required = true;
PFIC Asset Test Met (IRC § 1297(a)(2)).
Corporation qualifies as PFIC because 50% or more of its assets (by quarterly average value) produce or are held to produce passive income.
Assets tested include: investment securities, rental property, intangibles producing royalties, cash equivalents.
U.S. shareholders must file Form 8621 and choose taxation method.
Reference: IRC § 1297(a)(2), Form 8621
N N N
// Neither test met: not a PFIC pfic.is_pfic = false; pfic.form_8621_required = false;
Not a PFIC.
Foreign corporation does not meet either the income test or asset test for PFIC classification. No Form 8621 filing required.
Corporation is likely engaged in active business operations with primarily active (non-passive) income and assets.
Reference: IRC § 1297
Calculate PFIC taxation under the default excess distribution regime (IRC § 1291).
This is the harshest PFIC taxation method, applying when no QEF or mark-to-market election is in effect. Distributions exceeding 125% of the 3-year average are allocated across the holding period and taxed at the highest marginal rate, plus interest charge for tax deferral.
Also applies to gains on disposition of PFIC stock.
Reference: IRC § 1291, Form 8621 Part III
// Calculate excess distribution threshold (125% of 3-year average) pfic.excess_distribution_threshold = pfic.prior_3yr_avg_distribution * 1.25;
// Determine excess vs ordinary distribution if (pfic.current_year_distribution > pfic.excess_distribution_threshold) { pfic.excess_distribution = pfic.current_year_distribution - pfic.excess_distribution_threshold; pfic.ordinary_distribution = pfic.excess_distribution_threshold; } else { pfic.excess_distribution = 0.0; pfic.ordinary_distribution = pfic.current_year_distribution; }
Y excess_distribution > 0
// Allocate excess distribution across holding period if (pfic.excess_distribution > 0 && pfic.holding_period_years > 0) { // Allocate excess portion to each prior year pfic.excess_dist_per_year = pfic.excess_distribution / pfic.holding_period_years;
// Calculate tax at highest marginal rate (currently 37% for individuals, 21% for corps) // Using 37% as conservative estimate pfic.tax_per_year = pfic.excess_dist_per_year * 0.37;
// Total deferred tax (prior years only, exclude current year) pfic.total_deferred_tax = pfic.tax_per_year * (pfic.holding_period_years - 1);
// Interest charge on deferred tax (using underpayment rate, simplified to 5%) // Actual calculation uses IRS underpayment rate compounded over holding period double avg_deferral_years = pfic.holding_period_years / 2.0; pfic.interest_charge = pfic.total_deferred_tax * 0.05 * avg_deferral_years;
// Current year portion taxed at ordinary rates (also highest rate) pfic.current_year_tax = pfic.tax_per_year + (pfic.ordinary_distribution * 0.37);
// Total PFIC tax liability pfic.total_pfic_tax = pfic.total_deferred_tax + pfic.interest_charge + pfic.current_year_tax; } else { // No excess distribution: ordinary income treatment pfic.excess_dist_per_year = 0.0; pfic.total_deferred_tax = 0.0; pfic.interest_charge = 0.0; pfic.current_year_tax = pfic.ordinary_distribution * 0.37; pfic.total_pfic_tax = pfic.current_year_tax; }
pfic.income_from_pfic = pfic.current_year_distribution; pfic.tax_liability_from_pfic = pfic.total_pfic_tax;
Excess Distribution Regime - Default PFIC Taxation (IRC § 1291).
This harsh regime applies when shareholder has not made QEF or mark-to-market election.
Distributions exceeding 125% of the prior 3-year average are "excess distributions" allocated ratably to each year in the holding period. Tax on amounts allocated to prior years is calculated at the highest marginal rate, plus an interest charge for the deemed deferral.
The interest charge compensates the IRS for taxes that should have been paid earlier, using the underpayment rate compounded over the holding period.
This regime is intentionally punitive to encourage shareholders to make QEF elections.
Reference: IRC § 1291(a), (b), (c), Form 8621 Part III
Y excess_distribution = 0
// No distribution in current year pfic.excess_distribution = 0.0; pfic.ordinary_distribution = 0.0; pfic.total_pfic_tax = 0.0; pfic.income_from_pfic = 0.0; pfic.tax_liability_from_pfic = 0.0;
No PFIC Distribution Received.
No taxation occurs in current year if no distribution received and no stock disposition. However, future distributions may trigger excess distribution treatment.
Reference: IRC § 1291
N
// Not a PFIC: no PFIC taxation pfic.total_pfic_tax = 0.0; pfic.income_from_pfic = 0.0; pfic.tax_liability_from_pfic = 0.0;
Not a PFIC - No Excess Distribution Calculation.
Corporation does not qualify as PFIC, so excess distribution regime does not apply.
Reference: IRC § 1297
Apply Qualified Electing Fund (QEF) election under IRC § 1293.
QEF election allows U.S. shareholder to include pro-rata share of PFIC's earnings currently (similar to Subpart F), avoiding the harsh excess distribution regime and interest charge.
Requirements: - PFIC must provide annual information statement with ordinary earnings and net capital gain - Election made on Form 8621 - Can be "pedigreed" (made in first year) or "non-pedigreed" (made after first year)
Benefits: - No interest charge on deferred tax - Distributions of previously taxed amounts are tax-free - Gain on sale limited to appreciation after election
Reference: IRC § 1293, Form 8621 Part II
Y Y Y
// Calculate QEF inclusion (pro-rata share of PFIC earnings) pfic.qef_total_inclusion = pfic.qef_ordinary_earnings + pfic.qef_net_capital_gain;
// Ordinary earnings taxed as ordinary income // Net capital gain taxed as long-term capital gain (preferential rates)
// Previously taxed amounts increase shareholder's basis pfic.qef_previously_taxed_amount += pfic.qef_total_inclusion;
// Distributions reduce previously taxed amounts (tax-free to extent of prior inclusions) if (pfic.current_year_distribution > 0) { if (pfic.current_year_distribution <= pfic.qef_previously_taxed_amount) { pfic.qef_distribution_excluded = pfic.current_year_distribution; pfic.qef_previously_taxed_amount -= pfic.current_year_distribution; } else { pfic.qef_distribution_excluded = pfic.qef_previously_taxed_amount; pfic.qef_previously_taxed_amount = 0.0; } }
// Income inclusion for current year pfic.income_from_pfic = pfic.qef_total_inclusion;
// Tax calculated on ordinary earnings at ordinary rates, capital gain at preferential rates // Simplified: using 21% corporate rate for ordinary, 21% for capital gain (no preference for corps) pfic.tax_liability_from_pfic = pfic.qef_ordinary_earnings * 0.21 + pfic.qef_net_capital_gain * 0.21;
// Check if pedigreed QEF if (pfic.qef_election_year == pfic.first_year_as_shareholder) { pfic.pedigreed_qef = true; }
QEF Election - Current Inclusion of PFIC Earnings (IRC § 1293).
Shareholder includes pro-rata share of PFIC's ordinary earnings and net capital gain currently, similar to Subpart F treatment for CFCs.
Benefits: - Avoids harsh excess distribution regime and interest charge - Previously taxed inclusions create basis increase - Future distributions of previously taxed amounts are tax-free - Gain on sale limited to post-election appreciation
Requirements: - PFIC must provide annual information statement (PFIC Annual Information Statement) - Statement must report ordinary earnings and net capital gain - Election made on Form 8621
Pedigreed QEF: Election made in first year shareholder holds stock, no prior excess distribution regime history.
Non-pedigreed QEF: Election made after first year, may require deemed sale election or deemed dividend election to purge excess distribution regime taint.
Reference: IRC § 1293(a), (b), Treas. Reg. § 1.1295-1, Form 8621 Part II
