// High-taxed income excluded from tested income form_5471.tested_income = 0.0; form_5471.tested_loss = 0.0; form_5471.pro_rata_share_tested_income = 0.0;
// Track high-tax exclusion amount result.gilti_high_tax_exclusion += high_tax_income;
High-Tax Exclusion from GILTI (IRC § 951A(c)(2)(A)(i)(III))
Election to Exclude High-Taxed Income: U.S. shareholders may elect to exclude CFC income subject to foreign tax at an effective rate greater than 90% of the U.S. corporate tax rate.
Calculation of High-Tax Threshold: - U.S. corporate rate: 21% - 90% of U.S. rate: 18.9% - Foreign effective tax rate = Foreign taxes paid / Taxable income - If foreign rate > 18.9%, income can be excluded from GILTI
Rationale: High-taxed income is less likely to be the result of profit shifting to low-tax jurisdictions. Congress provided this exclusion to avoid double taxation (foreign tax + GILTI inclusion).
Election Requirements: - Made annually on a timely filed return (including extensions) - Made separately for each CFC - Applied on a tested unit basis (separate activities within CFC) - Once made, applies to all tested units with foreign rate > 18.9%
Impact: Excluded income is not included in tested income, reducing GILTI. However, the U.S. shareholder also cannot claim foreign tax credits for taxes paid on excluded income.
Reference: IRC § 951A(c)(2)(A)(i)(III) Reference: Treas. Reg. § 1.951A-2(c)(7) - High-tax exclusion Reference: Form 8992 Instructions - High-taxed income election
