// Step 1: Calculate net tested income result.gilti_net_tested_income = result.gilti_gross_tested_income - result.gilti_tested_loss;
// Step 2: Calculate GILTI inclusion // GILTI = Net Tested Income - Net Deemed Tangible Income Return result.gilti_inclusion = result.gilti_net_tested_income - result.gilti_net_deemed_tangible_income_return;
// GILTI cannot be negative (floor at zero) if (result.gilti_inclusion < 0.0) { result.gilti_inclusion = 0.0; }
GILTI Inclusion Calculation (IRC § 951A(b)(1))
GILTI Formula: GILTI = Net Tested Income - Net Deemed Tangible Income Return
Where: - Net Tested Income = Aggregate tested income of all CFCs MINUS aggregate tested losses of all CFCs - Net Deemed Tangible Income Return = 10% × Aggregate QBAI of all CFCs
Aggregation Across CFCs: U.S. shareholder combines tested income and QBAI from ALL CFCs. Tested income from one CFC can be offset by tested losses from another. QBAI from all CFCs is aggregated for the 10% deduction.
GILTI Floor at Zero: If NDTIR exceeds net tested income, GILTI is zero (not negative). Excess NDTIR cannot create a deduction; it is "wasted."
Example Calculation: CFC 1: Tested income = $1,000, QBAI = $2,000 CFC 2: Tested income = $500, QBAI = $1,000 CFC 3: Tested loss = ($200), QBAI = $500
Net Tested Income = $1,000 + $500 - $200 = $1,300 Aggregate QBAI = $2,000 + $1,000 + $500 = $3,500 NDTIR = 10% × $3,500 = $350 GILTI = $1,300 - $350 = $950
Inclusion in Gross Income: U.S. shareholder includes GILTI in gross income for the tax year. GILTI is treated as a dividend from the CFC (for foreign tax credit purposes) but does not reduce CFC's E&P.
Interaction with Subpart F: GILTI is calculated AFTER Subpart F inclusions. Subpart F income is excluded from tested income (no double inclusion).
Reference: IRC § 951A(b)(1) - GILTI amount Reference: Treas. Reg. § 1.951A-1(c) - GILTI inclusion Reference: Form 8992 - U.S. Shareholder Calculation of GILTI
