**Qualified Business Unit (QBU) Determination**
A foreign branch qualifies as a QBU when it: 1. Maintains separate books and records 2. Conducts a trade or business in a foreign country 3. Uses a functional currency other than the U.S. dollar 4. Has substantive operations (employees or physical office)
**Tax Consequences of QBU Status**:
1. **Foreign Currency Translation (IRC § 987)**: - Income and expenses translated at average exchange rate - Balance sheet items at spot rate - Translation gain/loss deferred until remittance - Remittance triggers recognition of deferred gain/loss
2. **Foreign Tax Credit - Separate Basket (IRC § 904(d)(1)(E))**: - Foreign branch income = separate limitation basket - Cannot mix with general basket or passive basket - Separate FTC limitation calculation required - Foreign taxes on branch income claimed in branch basket only
3. **Reporting Requirements**: - Form 8858 required (Information Return for Foreign Disregarded Entities) - Form 1118 Schedule H (foreign branch basket) - Must track branch E&P separately from CFC E&P
4. **Differences from CFC**: - No Subpart F income (branch income taxed currently) - No GILTI inclusion (GILTI applies to CFCs only) - No PTEP tracking (no deemed inclusions) - Branch assets not QBAI (QBAI applies to CFCs only)
**Common QBUs**: - Foreign sales office with local employees - Foreign manufacturing facility - Foreign service branch (consulting, engineering) - Foreign R&D center
**Not QBUs** (separate entity or insufficient activity): - Foreign subsidiary (separate corporation = potential CFC) - Minimal activity with no employees - Pass-through entity for holding investments - Entity using USD as functional currency
Reference: IRC § 989(a) Reference: Treas. Reg. § 1.989(a)-1 Reference: IRC § 904(d)(1)(E)
